"""Comma-sensitive asides and list boundaries.

A comma followed by a suffix, certain connectors, or a list's closing
punctuation gives a negative response.  Example-introducing phrases give a
positive response instead.  Formatting can separate a comma from its closing
bracket; recognizable TeX grouping cases receive weaker responses.
This captures selected boundary cues, not every token-specific response.
